const e=JSON.parse(`[{"position":"postmil","summary":"Postmillennialism places Christ's return after the millennium, which most modern proponents (Gentry, Mathison, Bahnsen) identify with the whole period between the two advents: Christ already reigns from heaven, Satan is bound at the first coming, and the gospel progressively disciples the nations until the world is largely Christianized. An older Puritan and Edwardsean variant reserves the millennium for a future 'latter-day glory' near the end of the church age. Either way, there is one Second Coming, one general resurrection and one judgment at the close of history.","events":[{"id":"cross-victory","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross and resurrection: decisive victory","at":0,"description":"Postmillennialists see the cross and resurrection as the definitive defeat of Satan and the enthronement of Christ, the ground for confidence that his kingdom will grow until it fills the earth (the stone of Daniel 2 that becomes a mountain).","texts":["John 12:31-32","Colossians 2:15","Daniel 2:34-35","Matthew 28:18-20"]},{"id":"millennium-interadvent","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: Christ's reign through the gospel","at":0,"end":90,"description":"Mainstream modern postmillennialism (Gentry, Mathison) reads the 'thousand years' of Revelation 20 as a symbolic, lengthy era spanning the whole church age, marked by gradual, often uneven but real gospel growth. Older postmillennialists such as Jonathan Edwards and Charles Hodge placed a distinct golden age of the millennium in the latter part of this period, before Christ's return.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","Psalm 110:1-2","1 Corinthians 15:24-26","Matthew 13:31-33"]},{"id":"satan-bound-first-advent","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Satan bound so the nations can be reached","at":1,"description":"The binding of Revelation 20:3 is read as Christ's plundering of the 'strong man' at his first coming: Satan is restrained specifically from deceiving the nations, so that the Great Commission can succeed.","texts":["Matthew 12:28-29","Revelation 20:2-3","Luke 10:17-18"]},{"id":"pentecost-kingdom-advance","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost: the kingdom's worldwide mission begins","at":2,"description":"The outpouring of the Spirit launches the discipling of the nations; Peter cites Psalm 110 to say Christ now reigns until his enemies are made his footstool.","texts":["Acts 2:16-21","Acts 2:33-36"]},{"id":"great-tribulation-66-70","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Great Tribulation (AD 66–70)","at":9,"end":10,"description":"Most contemporary postmillennialists are partial preterists and locate the Great Tribulation of the Olivet Discourse in the Jewish War, within 'this generation'. Historicist postmillennialists (many Puritans) instead spread tribulation imagery across church history.","texts":["Matthew 24:15-21","Matthew 24:34","Luke 21:20-24"]},{"id":"ad70-judgment","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem","at":10,"description":"The destruction of the temple is read as Christ's judgment-coming on covenant-breaking Jerusalem, vindicating him and closing the old covenant era. It is not the Second Coming.","texts":["Matthew 23:36-38","Matthew 24:30","Hebrews 8:13"]},{"id":"israel-ingathering","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"Large-scale conversion of the Jewish people","at":70,"description":"Many postmillennialists, following the Puritans (as surveyed by Iain Murray), expect a future mass conversion of ethnic Israel to Christ, bringing 'life from the dead' and a further flourishing of the gospel among the nations. Not all modern postmillennialists hold this.","texts":["Romans 11:12","Romans 11:15","Romans 11:25-26"]},{"id":"brief-rebellion","milestone":"apostasy","label":"Satan loosed: brief final rebellion","at":88,"end":90,"description":"At the end of the long era of gospel success, Satan is released for a 'little season' and stirs a short-lived revolt (Gog and Magog), which is ended by Christ's appearing.","texts":["Revelation 20:7-9","2 Thessalonians 2:8"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming","at":90,"description":"Christ returns once, visibly and bodily, to a largely Christianized world. Resurrection, judgment and the new creation follow as one complex of events; they are spaced out on this chart only for readability.","texts":["Acts 1:11","1 Corinthians 15:23-26","1 Thessalonians 4:16-17"]},{"id":"general-resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"General resurrection","at":91,"description":"One resurrection of the righteous and the wicked together at the last day.","texts":["John 5:28-29","Daniel 12:2","Acts 24:15"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Final judgment","at":92,"description":"All people stand before Christ at the great white throne.","texts":["Matthew 25:31-46","Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New heavens and new earth","at":100,"description":"The renewed creation is the final state. Some postmillennialists also see a present, covenantal 'new creation' begun in Christ (2 Corinthians 5:17), consummated here.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-5","2 Peter 3:13","Romans 8:19-23"]}],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism","boettner-millennium","murray-puritan-hope","edwards-history-redemption","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"position":"amil","summary":"Amillennialism (a name many proponents find unhelpful; some prefer 'realized millennialism') holds that the thousand years of Revelation 20 is the present age between Christ's two comings: Satan is bound, departed believers reign with Christ in heaven, and the church lives in a tension of gospel advance and ongoing tribulation. The age ends with a brief intensification of evil, then one Second Coming, one general resurrection, one judgment and the new creation (Hoekema, Riddlebarger, Storms, following Augustine).","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross, resurrection and ascension","at":0,"description":"Christ's death and exaltation inaugurate the 'already' of the kingdom; the last days have begun (Vos's two-age structure: the age to come has broken into this age).","texts":["Hebrews 1:2","Ephesians 1:20-22","1 Corinthians 10:11"]},{"id":"millennium-present","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: the present reign of Christ","at":0,"end":88,"description":"The thousand years symbolize the complete era between the advents. The 'first resurrection' is either regeneration or (more commonly today) the souls of believers living and reigning with Christ in heaven during the intermediate state. Revelation 20 recapitulates the age rather than following chapter 19 chronologically.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","Ephesians 2:5-6","John 5:24-25"]},{"id":"satan-bound","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Satan bound at the first advent","at":1,"description":"Satan is bound in a specific respect: he cannot prevent the gospel from going to the nations or gather them in a final assault before the appointed time.","texts":["Matthew 12:29","Revelation 20:3","John 12:31","Revelation 12:9-11"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The Spirit, the 'down payment' of the age to come, is given; the church is the one people of God from Jews and Gentiles.","texts":["Acts 2:16-21","Ephesians 1:13-14"]},{"id":"tribulation-age","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Tribulation throughout the age","at":2,"end":88,"description":"Amillennialists see tribulation as a mark of the whole church age (John is already 'in the tribulation'), with AD 70 as a terrible first-century instance and an intensification expected at the end. Views differ on how much of Matthew 24 refers to AD 70.","texts":["Revelation 1:9","John 16:33","Acts 14:22","Matthew 24:21-22"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem","at":10,"description":"Many amillennialists read the first part of the Olivet Discourse as fulfilled in AD 70, which also serves as a type of the final judgment; some (e.g. Jay Adams) take a more thoroughly preterist reading of Revelation.","texts":["Luke 21:20-24","Matthew 24:1-2"]},{"id":"elect-israel","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"'All Israel' saved","at":2,"end":88,"description":"Hoekema reads 'all Israel' in Romans 11:26 as the full number of elect Jews gathered throughout the age, shown here as a span. Others in the camp (Vos, Riddlebarger) expect a future large-scale turning of Jews to Christ before the end.","texts":["Romans 11:25-27","Galatians 6:16"]},{"id":"little-season","milestone":"apostasy","label":"Satan loosed: apostasy and the man of lawlessness","at":84,"end":88,"description":"Near the end, Satan is released for 'a little while'. Many amillennialists expect a final apostasy and a personal Antichrist (the man of lawlessness) who embodies the 'many antichrists' present in every age. Revelation 16:14, 19:19 and 20:8 are read as the same last battle.","texts":["Revelation 20:7-9","2 Thessalonians 2:3-8","1 John 2:18"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming","at":88,"description":"One visible, bodily return of Christ ends the age. Resurrection, judgment and new creation follow as one event complex; they are spaced out on this chart only for readability.","texts":["Matthew 24:30-31","1 Thessalonians 4:16-17","2 Thessalonians 1:7-10"]},{"id":"general-resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"General resurrection","at":89,"description":"Believers and unbelievers are raised together at 'the last day'.","texts":["John 5:28-29","John 6:39-40","Acts 24:15"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Final judgment","at":90,"description":"One judgment of all people, the great white throne.","texts":["Matthew 25:31-46","Revelation 20:11-15","2 Corinthians 5:10"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New heavens and new earth","at":100,"description":"Amillennialists such as Hoekema stress that the eternal state is an earthly, renewed creation, where Old Testament land promises find their ultimate fulfilment.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-4","2 Peter 3:10-13","Isaiah 65:17","Romans 8:21"]}],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come","augustine-city-of-god","vos-pauline-eschatology","riddlebarger-amillennialism-101","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"position":"histpremil","summary":"Historic premillennialism (Ladd, Blomberg, Grudem, and in an early form Papias, Justin Martyr and Irenaeus) holds that the kingdom is inaugurated now but that Christ returns once, after a future Great Tribulation the church passes through, to raise believers and reign on earth for a millennium before the final resurrection and judgment. Unlike dispensationalism, it sees one people of God and no pre-tribulation rapture.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross and exaltation: the kingdom inaugurated","at":0,"description":"Ladd's 'already/not yet': the kingdom of God has come in Jesus' ministry, death and resurrection, but awaits consummation in stages.","texts":["Mark 1:15","Luke 11:20","Acts 2:33-36"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The church, one people of God with Israel's believing remnant, begins its mission.","texts":["Acts 2:16-21","Ephesians 2:14-16"]},{"id":"church-age","milestone":"church-age","label":"Church age: kingdom present, Satan active","at":2,"end":76,"description":"Christ reigns from heaven and the kingdom advances, but Satan is not yet bound in the sense of Revelation 20 and evil persists until the end.","texts":["1 Peter 5:8","Matthew 13:24-30","1 Corinthians 15:25"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem","at":10,"description":"Ladd and Blomberg hold that the Olivet Discourse blends the near judgment on Jerusalem with the end of the age (prophetic foreshortening); AD 70 fulfils part of it and foreshadows the rest.","texts":["Luke 21:20-24","Mark 13:1-4"]},{"id":"great-tribulation","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Future Great Tribulation and Antichrist","at":76,"end":80,"description":"A final period of intense persecution under a personal Antichrist. The church is present and preserved through it rather than removed from it.","texts":["Matthew 24:21-31","2 Thessalonians 2:1-4","Revelation 13:5-7"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming (post-tribulational)","at":80,"description":"One glorious return 'after the tribulation of those days'. The 'rapture' is believers rising to meet the Lord in the air and escorting him back to earth, the sense Ladd gives to the Greek word apantēsis (a welcoming meeting).","texts":["Matthew 24:29-31","1 Thessalonians 4:15-17","Revelation 19:11-16"]},{"id":"first-resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"First resurrection: believers raised","at":80.5,"description":"The 'first resurrection' of Revelation 20:4-5 is a bodily resurrection of the saints at Christ's coming. Ladd argues the same verb (ezēsan, 'came to life') must mean bodily life for both groups in verses 4 and 5.","texts":["Revelation 20:4-6","1 Corinthians 15:23"]},{"id":"israel-turns","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"Israel looks on the one they pierced","at":80.5,"description":"Many historic premillennialists expect a national turning of Jews to Christ at or near his return, joining the one people of God.","texts":["Romans 11:26","Zechariah 12:10","Matthew 23:39"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: Christ reigns on earth","at":81,"end":95,"description":"Satan is bound and Christ reigns with his resurrected saints over a world in which mortal nations still live. Many treat 'a thousand years' as a long period rather than an exact count. Satan's binding begins here.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","1 Corinthians 15:24-25","Isaiah 11:6-9"]},{"id":"final-rebellion","milestone":"apostasy","label":"Satan loosed: Gog and Magog","at":95,"end":96,"description":"At the end of the thousand years Satan deceives the nations once more and is finally defeated.","texts":["Revelation 20:7-10"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Second resurrection and final judgment","at":97,"description":"The 'rest of the dead' are raised and judged at the great white throne.","texts":["Revelation 20:5","Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New heavens and new earth","at":100,"description":"The eternal state follows the millennium.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-5","2 Peter 3:13"]}],"sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","grudem-systematic","irenaeus-against-heresies","justin-dialogue-trypho","clouse-meaning-millennium"]},{"position":"disppremil","summary":"Dispensational premillennialism (Darby, Scofield, Walvoord, Pentecost, Ryrie, Ice) distinguishes Israel and the church. The church age is a 'parenthesis' inserted before Daniel's seventieth week; Christ raptures the church before a future seven-year tribulation, returns visibly at its end, and reigns for a literal thousand years from Jerusalem, fulfilling God's unconditional promises to national Israel. Progressive dispensationalists (Blaising, Bock) see more continuity, but keep the same broad sequence.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross: Messiah 'cut off'","at":0,"description":"The sixty-nine weeks of Daniel 9 end with Messiah's rejection and death; the prophetic clock for Israel then pauses (the 'gap' before the seventieth week).","texts":["Daniel 9:24-26","Luke 19:41-44"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost: the church begins","at":2,"description":"The church, a distinct body formed by Spirit baptism and not foreseen in Old Testament prophecy (a 'mystery'), begins at Pentecost.","texts":["Acts 2:1-4","Ephesians 3:3-6","1 Corinthians 12:13"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem (not the Great Tribulation)","at":10,"description":"Luke 21:20-24 describes AD 70, after which Jerusalem is 'trampled' until the times of the Gentiles end. Matthew 24:15-31, by contrast, is read as still future; 'this generation' is taken as the generation that sees those signs.","texts":["Luke 21:20-24","Daniel 9:26"]},{"id":"pretrib-rapture","milestone":"rapture","label":"Pre-tribulation rapture","at":70,"description":"Christ comes for his church, raising dead believers and catching up living ones, before the seventieth week begins. The church is promised deliverance from 'the wrath to come'. Midtribulation and prewrath variants place the rapture later in the week.","texts":["1 Thessalonians 4:13-18","1 Thessalonians 1:10","1 Corinthians 15:51-52","Revelation 3:10","John 14:2-3"]},{"id":"seventieth-week","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Seven-year tribulation (Daniel's 70th week)","at":70,"end":75,"description":"Antichrist confirms a covenant with Israel, then breaks it at the midpoint by setting up the abomination of desolation in a rebuilt temple. The final three and a half years are the Great Tribulation, the 'time of Jacob's trouble' (Revelation 6–19).","texts":["Daniel 9:27","Matthew 24:15-21","Jeremiah 30:7","Revelation 13:5","2 Thessalonians 2:3-4"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming to earth","at":75,"description":"Christ returns with his saints, defeats the beast's armies at Armageddon, and judges the living nations.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-21","Zechariah 14:3-4","Matthew 25:31-46"]},{"id":"israel-saved","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"National Israel saved and regathered","at":75,"description":"Surviving Israel turns to the Messiah and is regathered to the land, fulfilling the Abrahamic, Davidic and New covenant promises to the nation.","texts":["Zechariah 12:10","Romans 11:26-27","Ezekiel 36:24-28"]},{"id":"tribulation-saints-raised","milestone":"resurrection","label":"Old Testament saints and martyrs raised","at":75.5,"description":"Old Testament believers and tribulation martyrs are raised to share in the kingdom, completing the 'first resurrection', which dispensationalists see as occurring in phases (Christ, the church at the rapture, these saints).","texts":["Daniel 12:2","Revelation 20:4-6"]},{"id":"satan-bound","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Satan bound in the abyss","at":76,"description":"Satan is fully incapacitated for the thousand years; the binding is total, not partial.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-3"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennial kingdom","at":76,"end":94,"description":"A literal thousand-year reign of Christ on David's throne in Jerusalem, with Israel at the head of the nations; many expect a millennial temple (Ezekiel 40–48). It ends with Satan's release and a final rebellion.","texts":["Revelation 20:4-9","Isaiah 2:2-4","Isaiah 11:1-10","Ezekiel 40:1-4","Luke 1:32-33"]},{"id":"great-white-throne","milestone":"judgment","label":"Great white throne judgment","at":96,"description":"The unbelieving dead are raised and judged (the second resurrection).","texts":["Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New heavens and new earth","at":100,"description":"The eternal state; many dispensationalists hold that Israel and the church remain distinct even here, though progressive dispensationalists stress their unity.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-2","Revelation 21:12-14","Isaiah 66:22"]}],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","ryrie-dispensationalism","blaising-bock-progressive","hultberg-three-views-rapture","bock-three-views"]},{"position":"partial-preterism","summary":"Partial (orthodox) preterism holds that many prophecies, especially the Olivet Discourse's Great Tribulation and much of Revelation, were fulfilled in the first century, culminating in Christ's judgment-coming on Jerusalem in AD 70, while the bodily Second Coming, general resurrection and final judgment remain future. It is a hermeneutic combined with postmillennialism (Gentry, DeMar, Mathison) or amillennialism (Jay Adams); R. C. Sproul held it while leaving the millennial question more open. Much of its Revelation reading rests on a pre-70 date, which is disputed.","events":[{"id":"cross-seventy-weeks","milestone":"cross","label":"Seventy weeks fulfilled in Christ","at":0,"description":"Daniel's seventy weeks run continuously, with no gap, to Christ's ministry and death, which 'make an end of sins' and confirm the covenant; Jerusalem's destruction follows as the consequence.","texts":["Daniel 9:24-27","Matthew 26:28"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost: the 'last days' of the old covenant","at":2,"description":"The 'last days' are the final generation of the old covenant order (AD 30–70), in which the gospel is preached to the known world before the end of that age.","texts":["Acts 2:16-21","Hebrews 1:2","Matthew 24:14","Colossians 1:6"]},{"id":"beast-nero","milestone":"antichrist","label":"The beast: Nero and Rome","at":8,"end":9.5,"description":"Many partial preterists identify the beast of Revelation 13 with Nero (666 matching a Hebrew spelling of 'Nero Caesar') and his persecution of AD 64–68; Gentry links the man of lawlessness and the apostasy with the same era and the Jewish revolt. Some partial preterists still expect a future personal Antichrist.","texts":["Revelation 13:5-7","Revelation 13:18","Revelation 17:9-11","2 Thessalonians 2:3-7"]},{"id":"great-tribulation","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Great Tribulation (AD 66–70)","at":9,"end":10,"description":"The Jewish War, with Roman armies surrounding Jerusalem, is the tribulation Jesus said would fall on 'this generation'. Josephus's account is cited for its severity.","texts":["Matthew 24:15-21","Matthew 24:34","Luke 21:20-24","Revelation 1:1-3"]},{"id":"ad70-coming","milestone":"ad70","label":"Christ's judgment-coming on Jerusalem","at":10,"description":"The 'coming on the clouds' of Matthew 24:30 and 26:64 is read, in Old Testament prophetic idiom (Isaiah 19:1), as Christ's vindication and enthronement displayed in judgment on the temple; the old covenant age ends. N. T. Wright reads the Olivet imagery similarly without adopting the label.","texts":["Matthew 24:29-30","Matthew 26:64","Isaiah 19:1","Daniel 7:13-14","Hebrews 8:13"]},{"id":"kingdom-age","milestone":"church-age","label":"Kingdom age (millennium for postmillennial preterists)","at":10,"end":90,"description":"After AD 70 the church carries on the mission of the established kingdom. Postmillennial preterists see the millennium of Revelation 20 here (they place its start at the first advent) and expect gospel success; amillennial preterists see the same period with more mixed expectations.","texts":["Revelation 20:4-6","Matthew 28:18-20","Revelation 11:15"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium (inter-advent)","at":0,"end":90,"description":"Most partial preterists read Revelation 20 as the present reign of Christ from the first advent to the second; whether it ends in near-universal gospel success (postmil) or not (amil) depends on the millennial view combined with this hermeneutic.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","1 Corinthians 15:24-26"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Future bodily Second Coming","at":90,"description":"Unlike full preterists, partial preterists insist on a future personal return. Many see a transition in the Olivet Discourse at Matthew 24:36 ('that day'), and read Acts 1:11 and 1 Corinthians 15 as still future.","texts":["Acts 1:11","Matthew 24:36","1 Corinthians 15:23-28","1 Thessalonians 4:16"]},{"id":"resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"Bodily resurrection","at":91,"description":"A future physical resurrection of all the dead, as confessed in the historic creeds.","texts":["John 5:28-29","1 Corinthians 15:42-54"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Final judgment","at":92,"description":"Universal judgment at the end of history.","texts":["Acts 17:31","Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"Consummated new creation","at":100,"description":"Some partial preterists read Revelation 21–22 as describing the new covenant order already begun after AD 70 (Gentry), while still expecting the final consummation of 2 Peter 3 and Romans 8.","texts":["2 Peter 3:10-13","Romans 8:19-23","Revelation 21:1-5"]}],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","sproul-last-days","demar-last-days-madness","wright-jesus-victory","gentry-ice-great-tribulation","adams-time-is-at-hand","gregg-revelation-four-views"]},{"position":"full-preterism","summary":"Full preterism, which its proponents call 'covenant eschatology' (Max King, Don Preston), holds that all prophecy, including the Second Coming, the resurrection and the judgment, was fulfilled by AD 70, when the old covenant age ended. Believers now live in the new creation, which has no end. This places it outside the historic creeds (the Apostles' and Nicene creeds confess a future return and bodily resurrection), and critics including partial preterists (Gentry, Mathison) argue it misreads 1 Corinthians 15 and Acts 1:11. Proponents reply that the creeds are subordinate to Scripture and that the New Testament's time texts ('soon', 'this generation') must govern every eschatological promise.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross: covenant transition begins","at":0,"description":"Christ's death inaugurates the new covenant, but the old covenant world continues alongside it for a final generation.","texts":["Hebrews 8:13","Hebrews 9:8-10"]},{"id":"satan-bound","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Satan bound","at":0.5,"description":"Satan is bound by Christ's work; full preterists typically see him loosed for the 'little season' of the Jewish revolt and then destroyed by AD 70 (Romans 16:20: 'soon').","texts":["Matthew 12:29","Romans 16:20","Revelation 20:2-3"]},{"id":"millennium-transition","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: the AD 30–70 transition","at":0,"end":10,"description":"Most full preterists (following Max King) take the thousand years as symbolic of the forty-year transition between covenants; a minority place it elsewhere (for example, from AD 70 onward or in the pre-Christian period).","texts":["Revelation 20:1-7","1 Corinthians 10:11"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost: the 'last days' of the old age","at":2,"description":"The 'last days' are the closing years of the old covenant age, not of world history.","texts":["Acts 2:16-20","Hebrews 1:1-2"]},{"id":"antichrist","milestone":"antichrist","label":"Antichrists and the beast","at":8,"end":9.5,"description":"The 'many antichrists' were first-century apostates, and the beast and man of lawlessness are first-century figures (often Nero or a Jewish leader).","texts":["1 John 2:18","2 Thessalonians 2:3-8","Revelation 13:18"]},{"id":"great-tribulation","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Great Tribulation (AD 66–70)","at":9,"end":10,"description":"Shared with partial preterism: the Jewish War fulfils Matthew 24.","texts":["Matthew 24:21","Matthew 24:34","Luke 21:22"]},{"id":"ad70-end-of-age","milestone":"ad70","label":"End of the age","at":10,"description":"The fall of the temple marks 'the end of the age' (Matthew 24:3): the old covenant world passes away, which proponents equate with 'heaven and earth' passing away.","texts":["Matthew 24:3","Matthew 24:35","Hebrews 12:26-28"]},{"id":"parousia","milestone":"parousia","label":"The Parousia","at":10,"description":"Christ's one Second Coming happened in AD 70, in judgment and in the vindication of his people, as the time texts are said to require. Critics point to Acts 1:11 ('in the same way') and the lack of a visible, bodily return; proponents read 'in the same way' as a coming in clouds of glory.","texts":["Matthew 16:27-28","Matthew 10:23","James 5:8-9","Revelation 22:20","Acts 1:11"]},{"id":"resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"Resurrection","at":10,"description":"Proponents most commonly take this as a corporate or covenantal resurrection: the people of God raised out of the 'death' of the old covenant into the life of the new (King's 'corporate body view'). Others hold an 'individual body at death' view, in which believers since AD 70 receive immortal bodies in heaven at death. Critics argue that 1 Corinthians 15 requires a future, physical resurrection.","texts":["1 Corinthians 15:51-54","Daniel 12:2","Daniel 12:7","Ezekiel 37:11-14","Acts 24:15"]},{"id":"judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Judgment","at":10,"description":"The judgment of the living and the dead is placed at AD 70. Many full preterists add that individuals are judged at death from then on.","texts":["Acts 24:25","2 Timothy 4:1","1 Peter 4:5-7","Revelation 11:18"]},{"id":"new-creation-covenantal","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New creation (covenantal), from AD 70 onward","at":10,"end":100,"description":"The new heavens and new earth are the new covenant order, fully established in AD 70 and enduring without end. No future end of physical history is expected; the gospel goes on spreading. Proponents differ on whether the physical cosmos itself will ever change.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-5","Isaiah 65:17-20","2 Peter 3:13","2 Corinthians 5:17"]}],"sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-eschatology-org","mathison-when-shall","gentry-pmw-hyper-preterism","nicene-creed"]},{"position":"historicism","summary":"Historicism, the dominant Protestant reading from the Reformation to the nineteenth century (Luther, the Puritans, Isaac Newton, Albert Barnes, E. B. Elliott), reads Revelation and Daniel as a continuous prophetic chart of church history from the apostles to the end, usually applying the 'day-year principle' (1,260 days = 1,260 years) and identifying the papacy as Antichrist. The specific dates and identifications below follow common nineteenth-century schemes; proponents differed widely on details. Historicists were both postmillennial (most Puritans, Edwards) and premillennial (Elliott, Spurgeon); this chart follows the postmillennial pattern and notes the alternative.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross: the Lamb takes the scroll","at":0,"description":"Christ's victory qualifies him to open the sealed scroll of history (Revelation 5); Daniel's seventy weeks are usually fulfilled in his ministry.","texts":["Revelation 5:5-10","Daniel 9:24-27"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The apostolic church begins the history Revelation will chart.","texts":["Acts 2:1-4"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem","at":10,"description":"Most historicists read much of the Olivet Discourse as fulfilled in AD 70, but date Revelation c. AD 95 (a disputed date), so its visions begin after Jerusalem's fall.","texts":["Luke 21:20-24","Matthew 24:34"]},{"id":"seals-trumpets","milestone":"other","label":"Seals and trumpets: pagan Rome to the Turks","at":10,"end":45,"description":"In Elliott's widely followed scheme the seals trace pagan Rome from prosperity to Constantine (c. 312); the first four trumpets are the barbarian invasions that toppled the western empire (c. 400–476); the fifth and sixth are the Saracen and Ottoman Turkish woes, ending with the fall of Constantinople (1453).","texts":["Revelation 6:1-17","Revelation 8:6-13","Revelation 9:1-21"]},{"id":"papal-antichrist","milestone":"antichrist","label":"1,260 years: the papal Antichrist","at":22,"end":54,"description":"Using the day-year principle, the 1,260 days, 42 months and 'time, times and half a time' are read as 1,260 years of papal dominance. A common reckoning ran from Justinian's decree (533/538) to the French capture of Pope Pius VI (1798); others proposed 606–1866. The Westminster Confession (1646) named the pope as the man of sin. The papal power's persecution of faithful believers is this view's Great Tribulation.","texts":["Daniel 7:25","Revelation 13:5-7","2 Thessalonians 2:3-4","Revelation 11:2-3","Ezekiel 4:6"]},{"id":"reformation","milestone":"other","label":"Reformation: the little open book and the two witnesses","at":47,"description":"The angel with the open book (Revelation 10) is read as the recovery of the Bible in the Reformation (1517), and the witnesses' resurrection (Revelation 11) as the revival of Protestant testimony.","texts":["Revelation 10:1-11","Revelation 11:3-12"]},{"id":"vials","milestone":"other","label":"Bowls (vials): judgments on the papal and Ottoman powers","at":54,"end":62,"description":"Many nineteenth-century historicists saw the bowls poured out from the French Revolution onward, with the drying of the Euphrates as the Ottoman empire's decline. Historicists today usually place the present near the end of the sequence.","texts":["Revelation 16:1-21","Revelation 16:12"]},{"id":"jews-converted","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"Conversion of the Jews","at":65,"description":"Puritan historicists widely expected a future national conversion of the Jews, which would spark worldwide revival, a hope that drove early Protestant missions (Iain Murray's 'Puritan Hope').","texts":["Romans 11:12","Romans 11:25-26","Hosea 3:4-5"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: the latter-day glory","at":68,"end":89,"description":"Postmillennial historicists (Edwards, many Puritans) expected a future era of gospel triumph after Antichrist's fall, before Christ returns. Premillennial historicists (Elliott, Guinness, Spurgeon) instead placed the Second Coming at its start. Some earlier historicists (and Augustine-influenced Reformers) read the millennium as already past or present.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","Isaiah 2:2-4","Habakkuk 2:14"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming","at":90,"description":"One personal return of Christ, after a brief final apostasy (postmillennial historicism), with the general resurrection.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-16","Revelation 20:7-9","1 Thessalonians 4:16"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Resurrection and final judgment","at":92,"description":"General resurrection and the great white throne.","texts":["Revelation 20:11-15","John 5:28-29"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New Jerusalem","at":100,"description":"The eternal state and the bride of the Lamb.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-4","Revelation 22:1-5"]}],"sources":["elliott-horae","barnes-notes-revelation","murray-puritan-hope","edwards-history-redemption","westminster-confession","gregg-revelation-four-views"]},{"position":"futurism","summary":"Futurism reads most of Revelation (chapters 4–22) and the final week of Daniel's seventy weeks as describing events still to come at the end of the age. It is associated with dispensationalism (Walvoord, Pentecost, Ryrie) but is not restricted to it: historic premillennialists such as Ladd are futurists, as are prewrath advocates (Rosenthal, Kurschner). Early Church Fathers (Irenaeus, Hippolytus) and the Jesuit Francisco Ribera (16th century) also read Revelation's climax as future. This chart shows the most common (pre-tribulational) futurist sequence.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross","at":0,"description":"Christ's first coming fulfils the first sixty-nine weeks of Daniel 9 and secures redemption.","texts":["Daniel 9:25-26","Revelation 5:9"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The church age begins.","texts":["Acts 2:1-4"]},{"id":"church-age","milestone":"church-age","label":"Church age (Revelation 1–3)","at":2,"end":70,"description":"Revelation 1:19 is read as a three-part outline: what John saw, 'the things that are' (the seven churches, chapters 2–3), and 'what will take place after this' (chapters 4–22). Some futurists also see the seven letters as a preview of church-age eras.","texts":["Revelation 1:19","Revelation 2:1-3:22","Revelation 4:1"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"Fall of Jerusalem: a partial, near fulfilment","at":10,"description":"Futurists generally grant that Luke 21:20-24 describes AD 70, but argue that the cosmic signs, the worldwide scope and the visible return in Matthew 24 were not fulfilled then. Some see AD 70 as a preview of the end.","texts":["Luke 21:20-24","Matthew 24:29-31"]},{"id":"rapture","milestone":"rapture","label":"Rapture (timing debated)","at":70,"description":"Most popular futurists hold a pre-tribulation rapture, sometimes linked with John being called up in Revelation 4:1; mid-tribulation, prewrath (before the Day of the Lord, within the week) and post-tribulation futurists place it later.","texts":["1 Thessalonians 4:16-17","Revelation 3:10","Revelation 4:1","Matthew 24:31"]},{"id":"tribulation","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Future tribulation (Revelation 6–19)","at":70,"end":75,"description":"The seals, trumpets and bowls describe literal, global judgments during a final seven-year period (Daniel's seventieth week), with the Great Tribulation proper in its second half.","texts":["Daniel 9:27","Revelation 6:1-17","Revelation 16:1-21","Matthew 24:21"]},{"id":"antichrist","milestone":"antichrist","label":"Future Antichrist and rebuilt temple","at":72.5,"end":75,"description":"A future world ruler (the beast) breaks a covenant with Israel at the midpoint, sets up the abomination of desolation and demands worship for 42 months.","texts":["Revelation 13:1-18","2 Thessalonians 2:3-4","Daniel 9:27","Matthew 24:15"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Visible return (Revelation 19)","at":75,"description":"Christ returns to earth, destroys the beast and false prophet, and begins his reign.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-21","Zechariah 14:4"]},{"id":"israel","milestone":"israel-conversion","label":"Israel's national repentance","at":75,"description":"A future mass turning of Israel to Christ at his appearing, held by most futurists, dispensational or not.","texts":["Zechariah 12:10","Romans 11:26","Revelation 7:4-8"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium follows the return","at":76,"end":94,"description":"Reading Revelation 19–20 in sequence, futurists place the binding of Satan and the thousand-year reign after the Second Coming.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Great white throne","at":96,"description":"After Satan's last rebellion, the dead are judged.","texts":["Revelation 20:7-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New heavens and new earth","at":100,"description":"Revelation 21–22 describes the eternal state.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-22:5"]}],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","ladd-commentary-revelation","rosenthal-prewrath","kurschner-antichrist-before-dotl","pate-four-views-revelation","gregg-revelation-four-views"]},{"position":"idealism","summary":"Idealism (also called the spiritual or symbolic approach) reads Revelation not as a code for particular dated events but as a symbolic portrayal of the timeless conflict between Christ and Satan, the church and the world, which recurs throughout the whole age. The visions repeat this conflict in parallel cycles (Hendriksen's 'progressive parallelism'), each ending with judgment and victory. Most idealists still affirm a real future consummation; this chart therefore shows mostly spans covering the whole church age, followed by the end. Pure idealism (e.g. William Milligan) gives less weight to any single final period than modified forms do.","events":[{"id":"lamb-victory","milestone":"cross","label":"The Lamb's victory","at":0,"description":"The slain and standing Lamb (Revelation 5) is the key to the book: the decisive battle is already won, and every later vision is read in its light.","texts":["Revelation 5:5-6","Revelation 12:10-11"]},{"id":"satan-cast-down","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Dragon cast down and bound","at":0.5,"description":"Revelation 12 (the dragon thrown down) and Revelation 20 (the dragon bound) are parallel portrayals of Satan's defeat at Christ's first coming.","texts":["Revelation 12:7-12","Revelation 20:1-3","John 12:31"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: the reign of the saints","at":0,"end":90,"description":"The thousand years symbolize the completeness of Christ's reign and of the martyrs' vindication during the whole age; idealists are usually amillennial.","texts":["Revelation 20:4-6"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The church, symbolized by the lampstands and the woman of Revelation 12, begins its witness in the wilderness.","texts":["Revelation 1:20","Revelation 12:6","Revelation 12:14"]},{"id":"tribulation-recurring","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Tribulation in every generation","at":2,"end":90,"description":"The seals, trumpets and bowls are not consecutive stages but recurring patterns of war, famine, persecution and judgment that run through every era, each cycle ending at the final judgment.","texts":["Revelation 6:1-17","Revelation 8:6-9:21","Revelation 7:14"]},{"id":"beast-recurring","milestone":"antichrist","label":"The beast: persecuting power in every age","at":2,"end":90,"description":"The sea-beast stands for any state or political power that claims divine authority and persecutes the faithful; the land-beast (false prophet) is false religion and ideology serving it. Rome was the first readers' example, but not the only one.","texts":["Revelation 13:1-18","1 John 2:18","Daniel 7:3-7"]},{"id":"babylon-recurring","milestone":"other","label":"Babylon the harlot: the seductive world-system","at":2,"end":90,"description":"Babylon represents the world's economic and cultural seduction in every age, and the call to 'come out of her' applies in every generation.","texts":["Revelation 17:1-6","Revelation 18:1-4"]},{"id":"ad70","milestone":"ad70","label":"AD 70 as one instance","at":10,"description":"Idealists do not tie Revelation to Jerusalem's fall; most date it c. AD 95 (a disputed date). Judgments such as AD 70 are treated as historical examples of the pattern the book reveals.","texts":["Matthew 24:1-2"]},{"id":"final-intensification","milestone":"apostasy","label":"Final intensification","at":85,"end":90,"description":"Many idealists, Hendriksen among them, see the cycles growing more intense toward the end, with Satan's 'little season' and a final assault on the church.","texts":["Revelation 20:7-9","Revelation 16:14-16","2 Thessalonians 2:3"]},{"id":"consummation","milestone":"parousia","label":"Christ's return and consummation","at":90,"description":"The rider on the white horse ends the conflict once for all. Each parallel cycle of visions closes here.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-16","Revelation 11:15-18","Revelation 14:14-20"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Final judgment","at":91,"description":"The general resurrection and the great white throne.","texts":["Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New Jerusalem","at":100,"description":"The goal toward which every cycle points: God dwelling with his people.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-5","Revelation 22:1-5"]}],"sources":["hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","gregg-revelation-four-views","pate-four-views-revelation","hoekema-bible-future"]},{"position":"eclectic","summary":"The eclectic (or 'redemptive-historical idealist') approach, defended by G. K. Beale and Dennis Johnson and used in varying ways by Craig Keener and Grant Osborne, combines the strengths of the other approaches. Revelation spoke to first-century churches about Rome (preterist), portrays transhistorical patterns of the whole age in parallel cycles (idealist), and climaxes in a real future consummation (futurist). Beale is amillennial; Osborne, a more futurist-leaning eclectic, is premillennial. This chart follows Beale's amillennial version.","events":[{"id":"cross","milestone":"cross","label":"Cross: the Lamb's victory inaugurates the end","at":0,"description":"Beale stresses inaugurated eschatology: the 'latter days' of Daniel began with Christ's death and resurrection (Revelation 1:1 echoes Daniel 2:28-29).","texts":["Revelation 1:1","Daniel 2:28-29","Revelation 5:9-10"]},{"id":"satan-bound","milestone":"satan-bound","label":"Satan bound","at":1,"description":"Satan is restrained from deceiving the nations so that the gospel can spread, in parallel with his casting down in Revelation 12.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-3","Revelation 12:7-12"]},{"id":"millennium","milestone":"millennium","label":"Millennium: the inter-advent age","at":0,"end":88,"description":"Revelation 20 recapitulates the church age; the saints reign with Christ from heaven while the church suffers on earth.","texts":["Revelation 20:4-6"]},{"id":"pentecost","milestone":"pentecost","label":"Pentecost","at":2,"description":"The church, the true Israel and a kingdom of priests, begins its prophetic witness (the two witnesses as the church).","texts":["Revelation 1:6","Revelation 11:3-7"]},{"id":"parallel-cycles","milestone":"church-age","label":"Parallel cycles of visions","at":2,"end":88,"description":"Seals, trumpets and bowls recapitulate the same period from different angles, each with a first-century reference point and an end-time climax.","texts":["Revelation 6:12-17","Revelation 11:15-19","Revelation 16:17-21"]},{"id":"tribulation","milestone":"great-tribulation","label":"Tribulation already and not yet","at":2,"end":88,"description":"The church is already in the tribulation (Revelation 1:9), which climaxes in a final, more intense persecution before Christ returns.","texts":["Revelation 1:9","Revelation 7:14","Daniel 12:1"]},{"id":"ad70-rome","milestone":"ad70","label":"First-century setting","at":10,"description":"The first readers would have recognized Rome and the imperial cult in the beast and Babylon. Beale dates Revelation c. AD 95 (the date is disputed) and does not make AD 70 the book's main subject, though the Olivet Discourse's near fulfilment is widely acknowledged.","texts":["Revelation 17:9","Revelation 2:13","Luke 21:20-24"]},{"id":"antichrist-final","milestone":"antichrist","label":"Final Antichrist embodiment","at":84,"end":88,"description":"The beast was embodied in Rome and recurs in later persecuting powers, culminating in a final antichristian manifestation at the end of history.","texts":["Revelation 13:1-8","2 Thessalonians 2:3-8","1 John 2:18"]},{"id":"second-coming","milestone":"parousia","label":"Second Coming","at":88,"description":"The one return of Christ, the final battle of Revelation 16, 19 and 20 seen from three angles.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-21","Revelation 20:7-10","Revelation 16:14-16"]},{"id":"resurrection","milestone":"resurrection","label":"General resurrection","at":89,"description":"One bodily resurrection of all at the end.","texts":["John 5:28-29","Revelation 20:12-13"]},{"id":"final-judgment","milestone":"judgment","label":"Final judgment","at":90,"description":"The great white throne judgment.","texts":["Revelation 20:11-15"]},{"id":"new-creation","milestone":"new-creation","label":"New creation as cosmic temple","at":100,"description":"Beale reads the New Jerusalem as the end-time temple filling the whole renewed creation, completing the Eden mandate.","texts":["Revelation 21:1-3","Revelation 21:22","Revelation 22:1-5"]}],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","johnson-triumph-of-the-lamb","pate-four-views-revelation","gregg-revelation-four-views"]}]`),t=JSON.parse(`{"start":"q-all-fulfilled","nodes":[{"id":"q-all-fulfilled","question":"Were all biblical prophecies, including the Second Coming and the bodily resurrection, fulfilled by AD 70?","explanation":"Full preterists answer yes because of the New Testament's time texts: Jesus promised some standing there would see him coming in his kingdom (Matthew 16:28), said 'this generation' would not pass until all was fulfilled (Matthew 24:34), and Revelation says its events are 'near'. They argue that splitting one coming into two is arbitrary. Everyone else, including partial preterists, answers no. They point to the visible, bodily return promised in Acts 1:11 and the physical resurrection described in 1 Corinthians 15, a hope confessed by every historic creed, and argue that these texts describe events with no first-century counterpart. Note that saying yes places a view outside historic creedal orthodoxy, which proponents accept on the grounds that Scripture outranks the creeds.","texts":["Matthew 16:27-28","Matthew 24:34","Revelation 22:20","Acts 1:11","1 Corinthians 15:20-26","2 Timothy 2:17-18"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: everything was fulfilled in the first century","result":"r-full-preterist"},{"label":"No: at least Christ's bodily return and the resurrection are still future","next":"q-millennium-future"}]},{"id":"q-millennium-future","question":"Is the thousand years of Revelation 20 a future reign of Christ on earth, beginning after he returns?","explanation":"Premillennialists read Revelation 19 and 20 in sequence: Christ returns (Revelation 19:11-21), then Satan is bound and the saints 'came to life' and reign (20:1-6). They note that the same verb describes both resurrections in 20:4-5, so if the second is bodily the first should be too, and that the earliest Fathers (Papias, Justin, Irenaeus) were chiliasts. Amillennialists and postmillennialists answer no. They read Revelation as cycles that recapitulate the age, so chapter 20 returns to the first advent; they note that Satan's binding echoes Matthew 12:29, and that elsewhere the New Testament presents one resurrection and judgment 'at the last day' (John 5:28-29), leaving no room for a thousand-year gap.","texts":["Revelation 19:11-21","Revelation 20:1-6","Matthew 12:29","John 5:28-29","1 Corinthians 15:23-24"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: Christ returns first, then reigns on earth","next":"q-return-stages"},{"label":"No: the millennium is the present age (or a stage within it) before Christ returns","next":"q-christianize"}]},{"id":"q-return-stages","question":"Is Christ's return one event, or two stages (a rapture of the church, then a later return to earth)?","explanation":"Pre-tribulationists see two stages: in 1 Thessalonians 4 the Lord comes for his saints and takes them to the Father's house (John 14:3), while in Revelation 19 he comes with them to earth. The church is promised exemption from wrath (1 Thessalonians 5:9; Revelation 3:10), and the church is not mentioned on earth in Revelation 4–18. Prewrath and midtribulation advocates also see a rapture before the end, but place it within the final seven years, before God's wrath proper begins. Post-tribulationists see one event: Matthew 24:29-31 places the gathering of the elect 'after the tribulation', and the word for 'meeting' (apantēsis) suggests going out to welcome a dignitary and escorting him back.","texts":["1 Thessalonians 4:15-17","1 Thessalonians 5:9","Revelation 3:10","John 14:2-3","Matthew 24:29-31","2 Thessalonians 2:1-3"],"options":[{"label":"One event, after the tribulation","next":"q-revelation-history-premil"},{"label":"Two stages: the church is raptured before the tribulation","next":"q-israel-distinct"},{"label":"Two stages: the rapture comes partway through the final seven years (midtribulation or prewrath)","result":"r-prewrath"}]},{"id":"q-revelation-history-premil","question":"Does Revelation chart the course of church history in sequence, from the apostles to the end?","explanation":"Historicist premillennialists (E. B. Elliott, H. Grattan Guinness, and in many sermons C. H. Spurgeon) read the seals, trumpets and bowls as successive eras, usually applying the day-year principle (Ezekiel 4:6) to the 1,260 days. Futurist historic premillennialists (George Ladd, Craig Blomberg) read most of chapters 4–22 as the climactic events at the end of the age, arguing that the historicist identifications kept changing and could be read only by those with access to European history.","texts":["Revelation 1:19","Revelation 11:2-3","Revelation 13:5","Ezekiel 4:6","Daniel 7:25"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: it is a continuous chart of church history","result":"r-historicist-premil"},{"label":"No: most of it describes a final period still to come","result":"r-historic-premil"}]},{"id":"q-israel-distinct","question":"Is Israel a distinct people of God, with future promises (land, throne, national restoration) separate from the church?","explanation":"Classic and revised dispensationalists (Darby, Scofield, Walvoord, Ryrie) see Israel and the church as two peoples with distinct programs; the church is a 'mystery' (Ephesians 3) not foreseen by the prophets, and God's unconditional covenant promises to Israel must be fulfilled to the nation. Progressive dispensationalists (Blaising, Bock, Saucy) still expect a future for national Israel, but see one unfolding kingdom program in which the church already shares the Davidic and New Covenant blessings, so that Israel and the church are distinct but not separate. (Covenant theologians and progressive covenantalists would see the church as the fulfilment of Israel, but few of them hold a pre-tribulation rapture.)","texts":["Romans 11:25-29","Jeremiah 31:31-37","Ephesians 3:4-6","Acts 15:14-17","Acts 1:6-7"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: Israel and the church are two peoples with distinct programs","result":"r-disp-pretrib"},{"label":"Israel has a national future, but the church shares already in one kingdom program","result":"r-progressive-disp"}]},{"id":"q-christianize","question":"Will the gospel substantially Christianize the nations before Christ returns?","explanation":"Postmillennialists answer yes. They read the Great Commission as a command to disciple nations, not just individuals, and point to kingdom parables of growth (the mustard seed and the leaven), the stone that fills the earth (Daniel 2), Christ reigning until all enemies are subdued (Psalm 110; 1 Corinthians 15:25), and prophecies of the earth filled with the knowledge of the Lord. Amillennialists answer no. They see the wheat and tares growing together until the harvest, expect suffering as the church's normal lot until the end, and note warnings of a final apostasy and of lawlessness increasing (2 Thessalonians 2; 2 Timothy 3). Many still expect real gospel growth, but not a Christianized world.","texts":["Matthew 28:18-20","Matthew 13:31-33","Daniel 2:35","Psalm 110:1-2","Habakkuk 2:14","Matthew 13:24-30","2 Timothy 3:1-5","Luke 18:8"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: the gospel will prevail among the nations before the end","next":"q-tribulation-past-postmil"},{"label":"No: good and evil grow together, and the church suffers until Christ returns","next":"q-tribulation-past-amil"}]},{"id":"q-tribulation-past-postmil","question":"Did the Great Tribulation of Matthew 24 take place in AD 66–70?","explanation":"Partial preterists answer yes. Jesus was answering the disciples' question about the temple's destruction (Matthew 24:1-3), and 'this generation' (24:34) means his contemporaries, as it does elsewhere in Matthew. Luke's parallel (21:20) says 'Jerusalem surrounded by armies', and the 'coming on the clouds' is prophetic judgment language (Isaiah 19:1). Those who answer no argue that the cosmic signs, the worldwide gathering of the elect and the visible coming did not happen in AD 70, or that 'generation' can mean 'race' or the generation that sees the signs. Historicist postmillennialists such as the Puritans spread tribulation and Antichrist imagery across church history instead.","texts":["Matthew 24:1-3","Matthew 24:15-21","Matthew 24:34","Luke 21:20-24","Isaiah 19:1","Matthew 23:36"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: it was fulfilled in the Jewish War and the fall of Jerusalem","result":"r-postmil-preterist"},{"label":"No, or only partly","next":"q-revelation-history-postmil"}]},{"id":"q-revelation-history-postmil","question":"Does Revelation chart church history in sequence, toward a future era of gospel triumph?","explanation":"Puritan and later historicist postmillennialists (Thomas Brightman, John Owen, Jonathan Edwards) read Revelation as a chronological map: the fall of the papal Antichrist and the conversion of the Jews would usher in the millennial 'latter-day glory'. Others who expect the gospel to triumph read Revelation symbolically, as a portrait of Christ's victory throughout the age rather than as a timetable. B. B. Warfield, for example, read Revelation 20 as a picture of the intermediate state while holding postmillennial hopes on other grounds.","texts":["Revelation 20:1-6","Revelation 11:15","Romans 11:12","Romans 11:15"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: it maps church history, and the latter-day glory is still ahead","result":"r-historicist-postmil"},{"label":"No: it portrays Christ's victory symbolically, not as a timetable","result":"r-postmil-idealist"}]},{"id":"q-tribulation-past-amil","question":"Was the Great Tribulation of Matthew 24 fulfilled in AD 66–70?","explanation":"Some amillennialists (Jay Adams and others) read the Olivet Discourse and much of Revelation preteristically, as fulfilled in the first-century judgments on Jerusalem and Rome. Many others read Matthew 24 as a blend: AD 70 is a real fulfilment, but also a pattern of tribulation that characterizes the whole age and intensifies before the end. Still others regard the Great Tribulation as primarily an end-time event.","texts":["Matthew 24:15-22","Matthew 24:34","Revelation 1:9","Revelation 7:14","Mark 13:9-13"],"options":[{"label":"Yes: it was fulfilled in AD 66–70","result":"r-amil-preterist"},{"label":"Partly: AD 70 begins a pattern of tribulation that runs to the end","next":"q-revelation-structure-amil"},{"label":"No: it mainly lies at the end of the age","next":"q-revelation-structure-amil"}]},{"id":"q-revelation-structure-amil","question":"How does Revelation's sequence of visions relate to history?","explanation":"Idealist and eclectic amillennialists (William Hendriksen, G. K. Beale, Dennis Johnson) see 'progressive parallelism': seals, trumpets and bowls each retell the whole age from a different angle, ending at the final judgment. That is why Revelation 20 can return to the first advent. Many Reformation-era Protestants (Luther, and the Reformers who followed Augustine's present millennium) read Revelation historically, as a chart that names the papacy as Antichrist. Other amillennialists (for example Anthony Hoekema and Kim Riddlebarger) emphasize that the 'signs of the times' run throughout the age but culminate in a personal Antichrist and a final tribulation, reading much of the book's climax as still future.","texts":["Revelation 6:12-17","Revelation 11:15-18","Revelation 16:17-21","Revelation 20:1-3","2 Thessalonians 2:3-8"],"options":[{"label":"Parallel cycles: each retells the whole church age in symbols","result":"r-amil-idealist-eclectic"},{"label":"A chronological chart of church history","result":"r-amil-historicist"},{"label":"Mainly a picture of the end-time climax, with patterns that run through the age","result":"r-amil-futurist"}]}],"results":[{"id":"r-full-preterist","title":"Full preterism (covenant eschatology)","positions":["full-preterism"],"summary":"You read every eschatological promise as fulfilled when the old covenant age ended in AD 70: the Parousia, the resurrection (usually understood corporately or as believers receiving immortal bodies at death) and the judgment. The new creation is the ongoing new covenant order. Advocates include Max King and Don Preston. This view sits outside the historic creeds, which confess a future bodily return and resurrection. Critics, including many partial preterists, also argue from 1 Corinthians 15 and Acts 1:11; proponents respond that the creeds must yield to the New Testament's time texts."},{"id":"r-historic-premil","title":"Historic premillennialism (post-tribulational, futurist)","positions":["histpremil","futurism"],"summary":"You expect a future Great Tribulation that the church will pass through, followed by one glorious return of Christ, the resurrection of believers and an earthly millennial reign before the final judgment. The view sees one people of God and an inaugurated kingdom (already/not yet). It has roots in the second-century Fathers and modern defenders such as George Ladd, Craig Blomberg and Wayne Grudem."},{"id":"r-historicist-premil","title":"Historicist premillennialism","positions":["histpremil","historicism"],"summary":"You read Revelation as a chart of church history, typically using the day-year principle, and expect Christ to return before an earthly millennium. This was a major nineteenth-century Protestant view (E. B. Elliott's Horae Apocalypticae, H. Grattan Guinness, with C. H. Spurgeon sharing its premillennial and historicist outlook), and Seventh-day Adventism inherits a form of it."},{"id":"r-prewrath","title":"Premillennialism with a prewrath or midtribulation rapture","positions":["futurism","histpremil"],"summary":"You expect the church to face part of the final seven years, including the Antichrist's persecution, and to be raptured before God's wrath in the Day of the Lord. That is followed by Christ's return to earth and a millennial reign. Prewrath advocates (Marvin Rosenthal, Alan Kurschner) are futurist premillennialists. Some retain dispensational distinctives about Israel and others do not, so this result sits between the historic and dispensational forms."},{"id":"r-disp-pretrib","title":"Dispensational premillennialism (pre-tribulational, futurist)","positions":["disppremil","futurism","dispensationalism"],"summary":"You distinguish Israel and the church, expect the church to be raptured before a future seven-year tribulation (Daniel's seventieth week), and look for Christ's return to establish a literal thousand-year kingdom centred on restored national Israel. Classic and revised dispensationalists such as John Walvoord, J. Dwight Pentecost, Charles Ryrie and Thomas Ice defend this, citing a consistently literal reading of prophecy."},{"id":"r-progressive-disp","title":"Progressive dispensational premillennialism","positions":["disppremil","futurism","dispensationalism"],"summary":"You expect a pre-tribulation rapture, a future tribulation and an earthly millennium with a restored national Israel, but see the Davidic and New Covenant promises as already inaugurated in Christ and shared by the church. Israel and the church are distinct but not separate peoples of God. Representatives include Craig Blaising, Darrell Bock and Robert Saucy."},{"id":"r-postmil-preterist","title":"Postmillennialism + partial preterism","positions":["postmil","partial-preterism"],"summary":"You read the Olivet Discourse's tribulation and much of Revelation (dated before AD 70, a disputed date) as fulfilled in the fall of Jerusalem, and expect the gospel to disciple the nations over the long millennial age before Christ's one bodily return, the general resurrection and the final judgment. Leading modern representatives include Kenneth Gentry, Greg Bahnsen, Gary DeMar, Keith Mathison and Douglas Wilson."},{"id":"r-historicist-postmil","title":"Historicist postmillennialism (Puritan)","positions":["postmil","historicism"],"summary":"You read Revelation as a map of church history, with Antichrist's fall and a future conversion of the Jews ushering in a 'latter-day glory' of worldwide gospel success before Christ returns. This was the dominant hope of many Puritans and of Jonathan Edwards, and it drove the early modern missionary movement (as traced in Iain Murray's The Puritan Hope)."},{"id":"r-postmil-idealist","title":"Postmillennialism + idealist or eclectic reading","positions":["postmil","idealism","eclectic"],"summary":"You expect the gospel to triumph among the nations before Christ returns, but read Revelation symbolically, as a portrait of Christ's victory in the church age rather than a timetable. B. B. Warfield combined postmillennial optimism with a symbolic reading of Revelation 20, and Loraine Boettner's case rested more on the broad sweep of Scripture than on a particular scheme for Revelation."},{"id":"r-amil-preterist","title":"Amillennialism + partial preterism","positions":["amil","partial-preterism"],"summary":"You see the millennium as the present reign of Christ and read the Great Tribulation and much of Revelation as fulfilled in the first century, without expecting the nations to be Christianized before the end. Jay Adams's The Time Is at Hand is a classic statement. R. C. Sproul held partial preterism while remaining undecided between amillennialism and postmillennialism."},{"id":"r-amil-idealist-eclectic","title":"Amillennialism + idealist or eclectic reading","positions":["amil","idealism","eclectic"],"summary":"You read Revelation as parallel cycles that span the whole age between Christ's comings, rooted in a first-century setting and climaxing in a real final judgment. The millennium is Christ's present reign. Representatives include William Hendriksen (More Than Conquerors), G. K. Beale and Dennis Johnson."},{"id":"r-amil-historicist","title":"Amillennialism + historicism (Reformation pattern)","positions":["amil","historicism"],"summary":"You see the millennium as the present (or, for some Reformers, an already-past) age, while reading Revelation and Daniel as a chart of church history that identifies the papacy as Antichrist. This combination was common among sixteenth-century Reformers, including Luther, who followed Augustine on the millennium while applying the historicist reading to their own day."},{"id":"r-amil-futurist","title":"Amillennialism with a future climax","positions":["amil","futurism"],"summary":"You see the millennium as the present age and Satan as bound now, while expecting a final intensification: a personal Antichrist, a last tribulation and apostasy, then one Second Coming, resurrection and judgment. Anthony Hoekema, Kim Riddlebarger and Sam Storms represent this 'signs of the times' amillennialism, though most also use idealist or eclectic readings of Revelation's structure."}]}`),i={timelines:e,flowchart:t},{timelines:a,flowchart:n}=i;export{n as f,a as t};
